WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.100
Good morning, John!

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:05.260
Okay, say that you had to put a dollar amount on the value of a human life.

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:07.120
What would that dollar amount be?

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:09.140
The easy answer is that you can't do that.

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:13.200
It's immoral,  it's inconceivable, it's incalculable; the value of a human life!

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:20.800
But, you know we do it. The government of the United States says the minimum amount you can sell an hour of your life for is $7.80.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:23.020
And the average wages of a person over their entire lives,

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:24.680
that's about two million dollars.

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:27.500
So that's kind of the value of a human life.

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:30.460
But no, it's not, that's the value of their economic input,

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:33.820
wages are paid per hour, but they're just a surrogate for, you know,

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:35.540
value created, for stuff made.

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:36.400
So let's try another angle,

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:37.960
Raw materials,

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:42.400
really raw materials, raw organs is what I'm talking about.

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:44.980
Uncooked, sold on the black market,

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:47.200
if i sold everything from my eyeballs to my forearms,

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:51.020
I could rake in about $500,000

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:53.360
Not bad, I wouldn't have much fun with it,

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:56.420
'cause of how I wouldn't have eyeballs or forearms,

00:00:56.420 --> 00:00:59.800
but interestingly, $500,000 is also the number that the United States government

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:02.320
pays to the family of a fallen soldier.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:04.720
But by other measures, this is very low.

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:08.120
Unlike working, advertising pays you for doing nothing

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:10.580
just for the presence your eyeballs in front of a screen.

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:15.540
The average cost of serving a thirty second television advertisement to a thousand people is about $24.

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:21.180
Math math math! That's $2.88 per hour multiplied by the number of hours in your life,

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:25.600
if you sold all of them this way, that would be, again, about two million dollars.

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:31.100
So, if we only sold the waking hours, because advertisers do not have the ability to advertise to us in our sleep,

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:33.100
that would only be about 1.3 million dollars.

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:37.420
Online video advertisements on the other hand, can go for less than a tenth of that,

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:41.860
so there's other reasons besides the fact that I'm annoyed by them that we avoid pre-rolls,

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:45.100
we just think they undervalue this community's time!

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:49.900
Why is you time, watching this video, worth a tenth of the time of a person watching a tv show?

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:51.900
I don't know! I think that's dumb!

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:56.900
But none of this is how economists actually calculate the worth of a human life, or of human time.

00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:58.360
Basically, they do it one or two ways.

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:01.020
The first is to calculate the amount of money,

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:05.860
that a someone has to be paid extra, to do a job that has a certain risk of death.

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:08.940
So if there's a job and it's dangerous, like lumberjack or something,

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:12.340
and there's a one in one thousand chance that you're gonna die doing this job,

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:18.020
and you say ''I have to be paid ten thousand dollars more to do that job!'' then you have just valued your life.

00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:21.560
You have made the decision that your life is worth ten million dollars.

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:22.520
Statistically.

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:28.960
Using this measure, various departments within the U.S. government put a value on a human life of about six to nine million dollars.

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:32.100
The second way is to calculate not how much you would need to get paid to risk your life,

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:34.460
but how much you would actually pay to save your life.

00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:40.240
On the high end, this comes in at around a $130,000 for one year of high quality, non-painful life.

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:44.380
So if you'd extent someone's life by ten years and those will all be high quality, non-painful years,

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:46.380
that's worth about 1.3 million dollars.

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:50.700
And for 78 years of high quality life that comes out to a remarkably similar number,

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:52.240
ten million dollars!

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:55.580
And there ends the fact-y portion of the video, now we're onto the opinion-y.

00:02:55.580 --> 00:03:01.160
It might initially seem crass or inhuman to say that there is an economic value of a human life,

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:02.220
and that instead you should just say

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:05.740
''We should do whatever we can to prevent death in every situation!''

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:09.400
but neh... To me that represents a kind of blindness to the inevitability of death.

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:13.000
Death has been good enough for everyone who has ever lived... So far.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:16.260
It's not the moment of death where all the value get's created or lost,

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:21.540
to me, the value is in enabling the life, all of the finite moments that we have on this planet together.

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:23.540
It's not about preventing the death.

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:25.140
Which is gonna happen anyway.

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:29.120
John, this was supposed to be kind of a silly video and it ended up being a little bit heavy...

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:29.920
Sorry about that.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:33.360
You were fantastic on the Late Late Show, and we shall continue our discussion

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:37.700
of how to make online video strong and awesome as the years progress soon!

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:39.300
I'll see you on Tuesday.

